{
  "schema": "vela.canonical-hashing.v1",
  "format_id": "vela.canonical-json/v1",
  "doctrine": "Every content-addressed id in the protocol (vf_ finding, vev_ canonical event, vpr_ proposal, the snapshot hash, the event-log leaf) is the SHA-256 of the canonical JSON bytes of a value. Two implementations derive the same id from the same logical content only if their canonicalization is byte-identical. These vectors pin vela.canonical-json/v1 so any implementation can verify byte-for-byte: keys sorted lexicographically at every depth, no inter-token whitespace, UTF-8 preserved verbatim (NOT \\u-escaped), floats in serde_json/Ryu shortest-round-trip form (1.0 keeps its decimal point), array order preserved, non-finite floats rejected.",
  "scope": {
    "conforming_implementations": [
      "Rust: vela_protocol::canonical::to_canonical_bytes / to_canonical_string / sha256_canonical (crates/vela-protocol/src/kernel/canonical.rs)",
      "Python: conformance/readers/python/canonical.py -- json.dumps(sort_keys=True, separators=(',',':'), ensure_ascii=False)."
    ],
    "explicitly_out_of_scope": [
      "JavaScript and TypeScript number encoders use the ECMAScript numeric model and do not preserve the Rust distinction between 1 and 1.0. Current portable protocol objects therefore forbid non-integer numeric fields in content-address preimages unless their schema defines an exact string representation.",
      "Python default json.dumps WITHOUT ensure_ascii=False renders non-ASCII as \\uXXXX (e.g. amyloid-\\u03b2) and therefore does NOT conform. Any Python content-addressing path MUST pass ensure_ascii=False."
    ]
  },
  "vectors": [
    {
      "name": "object keys sort at every depth",
      "input": { "z": 1, "a": { "y": 2, "b": 3 }, "m": [ { "q": 4, "p": 5 } ] },
      "canonical": "{\"a\":{\"b\":3,\"y\":2},\"m\":[{\"p\":5,\"q\":4}],\"z\":1}",
      "sha256": "48dece5ad3195f95d131d1cb30bf238e35817f078c7bc60dc537f52386a5e705"
    },
    {
      "name": "unicode preserved verbatim (load-bearing: amyloid-beta)",
